  "overview": "The Holden Kingswood was Australia's definitive full-size family car and commercial workhorse from 1968 to 1985. Replacing the HR series with the all-new HK, the Kingswood moniker became an indelible piece of Australian cultural lexicon—celebrated in television ('Kingswood Country'), song, and outback folklore. Encompassing the budget Belmont, mainstream Kingswood, luxury Premier, sporty Monaro-derived options, surf-culture Sandman van/ute, and heavy-duty One Tonner cab-chassis, over 1.5 million examples were produced across HK, HT, HG, HQ, HJ, HX, HZ, and WB series.",
  "australian_market_notes": "Introduced in 1968, the HK series brought the first V8s to Holden (imported Chevrolet 307ci V8) alongside new safety features like dual-circuit brakes and an energy-absorbing steering column. The 1969 HT introduced Holden's very own locally cast and machined V8s: the 253ci and 308ci Red V8s. The 1971 HQ was the single most successful automotive platform in Australian history, with 485,650 units built on an all-new full-perimeter front subframe chassis with coil springs on all four wheels. In 1977, the HZ series standardized Radial Tuned Suspension (RTS), giving the full-size car world-class European ride and cornering grip. The platform survived as the WB commercial range until 1985.",
